   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In January 2021, the secretariat of the Palestinian General Federation of Trade Unions (PGFTU), successfully held its Sixth General Congress. A new leadership was elected and a work plan developed, with a focus on enhancinge organizing through innovative strategies.

PGFTU supported in conducting awareness-raising campaigns on OSH issues to ensure workplace safety and that appropriate measures are applied in the workplace to prevent the spread of COVID-19.

PGFTU conducted a scoping study on working conditions in the services sectors that employ women to support efforts to increase membership and establish bipartite dialogue and collective bargaining at sectoral level. PGFTU developed an evidence based organizing strategy to recruit women workers in private education and service sectors targeting women workers. ILO supported PGFTU to build the capacity of its leaders, through regional webinars/trainings implemented for the Arab Trade Union Organization in May, June, July and December 2020, and in February, June, and July 2021. The activities focused on the Impact of COVID-19 on workers and the role of trade unions during the crisis.

ITCILO conducted training for 80 trade union leaders on building trade union strength through social dialogue and collective bargaining (January/February 2021). 

ILO supported PGFTU to develop a trade union position paper to better influence the forthcoming tripartite social dialogue conference to overcome the devastating impact of the pandemic on the labour market.

ILO supported the development of a a scoping study on the working conditions in the service sectors to develop an organizing strategy to increase membership and promote establishment of bipartite dialogue and collective bargaining at sectoral level. The study was validated in a tripartite meeting (13 April 2021). Based on this, the ILO supported PGFTU technically and financially to  recruit women workers and promote bipartite collective bargaining through an evidence-based approach in administrative occupations in the private service sectors (e.g. secretaries, receptionists, and office clerks).</narrative>
   </description>
